# Line endings are governed here rather than by each developer's core.autocrlf,
# so the rules are identical for every contributor, every container, and CI.
#
# The principle: match the line ending to whatever PARSES the file, and pin it
# only when that parser actually cares. See CODING_GUIDELINES.md.

# Default: normalize to LF in the repository, check out native.
* text=auto

# Always LF. A POSIX interpreter parses these wherever they were authored, and a
# CRLF becomes part of the shebang -- "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M". Container
# entrypoints and scripts copied to Linux hosts make this load-bearing.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Always CRLF. cmd.exe mishandles LF around labels and goto.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# NOT pinned, deliberately: *.ps1. PowerShell 5.1 and 7 both read LF scripts,
# including pwsh on Linux, so there is nothing to protect against. text=auto
# gives every developer native endings. Please do not "fix" this.
